Rehabilitation of Stiff Person Syndrome Presenting
After Laminectomy: A Case Report.
Rajat Mathur (National Rehabilitation Hospital, Wash-
ington, DC, United States); Robert D. Bunning, MD,
Cynthia G. Pineda, MD, Nina Rajaei, MD.
Disclosures: R. Mathur, none.
Patients or Programs: A 39-year-old woman with worsening
spasticity after laminectomy.
Program Description: The patient had a history of insulin-
dependent diabetes mellitus and progressive low back pain with
increased tone in lower extremities for 3 years. Spine imaging
(computed tomography, serial magnetic resonance imaging) re-
vealed L5-S1 mild disk herniation with mild foraminal narrowing.
The patient underwent a L5-S1 laminectomy, and, after surgery, her
spasticity and neuropathic pain in the lower extremities increased.
Anti-GAD75 antibody titer was elevated (77.6, n /H110211.5), and simul-
taneous ﬁring of agonist-antagonist muscles of lower extremities in
electromyography conﬁrmed the diagnosis of stiff person syn-
drome. The patient received intravenous immunoglobulin over 5
days and oral baclofen. She was transferred to acute inpatient
rehabilitation with lower extremity pain, increased tone, and ﬂexion
contracture at the hips with difﬁcult ambulation.
S267PM&R Vol. 3, Iss. 10S1, 2011</seg>

<seg id="s0002" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">Setting: Acute inpatient rehabilitation hospital.
Results: After 2 weeks of acute inpatient rehabilitation, which
included daily repetitive passive range of motion, stretching, moist
heat, hydrotherapy, and use of a stretcher chair, the patient’s axial
and limb stiffness and functional mobility improved. She was able to
ambulate with a rolling walker from 20-150 ft and stair climbing
improved.
Discussion: Stiff person syndrome is a rare neurologic condition
characterized by symptoms of muscle stiffness and spasms usually
starting axially. The involuntary contractions of agonist and antag-
onist muscles caused by motor unit activity are the key clinical and
electrophysiological markers. Anti-GAD75 antibodies are positive
in 65% of cases and are associated with other autoimmune condi-
tions. The chronic, progressive, and variable nature of the disease
indicates the need for life-long management and an individualized
rehabilitation program.
Conclusions: Awareness of this rare condition in addition to a
comprehensive history and physical examination could avoid un-
necessary invasive procedures. This case illustrates that intensive
rehabilitation with physical therapy and occupational therapy can
improve the functional outcome and quality of life of individuals
with stiff person syndrome.</seg>
Poster 279
One Year Follow-up of Relationships of Body Mass
Index With Impairments, Functional Limitations,
and Disability in Subject Who Were Obese.
Monique B. Perry, MD (Clinical Center, National Insti-
tutes of Health, Department of Health and Human
Services, Bethesda, MD, United States); Naomi Lynn H.
Gerber, MD, Ching-yi A. Shieh, PhD, Monica C. Skarulis,
MD.
Disclosures: M. B. Perry, none.
Objective: To determine correlations between changes in body
mass index and impairments, physical function, and disability in a
1-year follow-up of subjects who were obese.
Design: Prospective longitudinal descriptive study.
Setting: Biomedical research facility.
Participants: 18 subjects with body mass index (BMI) from
30.0-64 kg/m
2 (mean, 39.6 kg/m 2); 4 men and 14 women; 27-63
years old (mean, 46 years old); all were mobile.
Interventions: Not applicable.
Main Outcome Measures: Human activity proﬁle (HAP) a
self-report of physical activity; Health Assessment Questionnaire
(HAQ) a measure of activities of daily living and health status; Pain
Disability Index; Knee Osteoarthritis Outcome Survey; Hip Osteo-
arthritis Outcome Survey; physical examination, including joint
range of motion; BMI; and timed get up and go (TUG).
Results: In a 1-year follow-up, there was no change in the number
of subjects with a functional TUG score (/H1102110 seconds). TUG
inversely correlates (P/H11021.05) with a change in hip external rotation (r
/H11005–.51). One fewer subject had normal HAQ score, and one more
subject had moderate limitations. Change in HAQ inversely corre-
lates (P/H11021.05) with a change in knee ﬂexion (r/H11005–.58). One more
subject was moderately active on the HAP adjusted activity score
(AAS), 2 fewer subjects were active, and 1 more subject was im-
paired. Change in HAP AAS correlates (P/H11021.05) with change in hip
abduction (r/H110050.43). One subject remains with a high level of
disability on the Pain Disability Index. Mean increase in BMI is
0.67/H110063.6 kg/m
2 and the mean decrease in AAS is –1.1/H110068.1.
Change in BMI inversely correlates (P/H11021.05) with change in the HAP
AAS (r /H11005– 0.58), change in hip ﬂexion (r /H11005– 0.64), hip extension
(r /H11005– 0.49), and change in the subscales of the Hip Osteoarthritis
Outcome Survey, including hip-related quality of life ( r /H11005– 0.48)
and hip pain (r /H11005– 0.54).
Conclusions: In a 1-year follow-up of this cohort with obesity,
the group increased its BMI. Increased BMI was signiﬁcantly corre-
lated with decreased activity level and increased hip pain and
disability. Further long-term studies are warranted to determine if
this correlation of function and disability persists over time.
Poster 280
Incidence of Missed Therapy Due to Adverse
Effects of Opioid Use: Nausea, Vomiting,
Constipation, and Lethargy.
Daniel Nemeth, MD (Marianjoy Rehabilitation Hospital,
Wheaton, IL, United States); Richard Krieger, MD.
Disclosures: D. Nemeth, none.
Objective: To determine the amount of missed therapy due to the
adverse effects of opioid use, including nausea, vomiting, constipa-
tion, and lethargy (illness).
Design: Retrospective case review over 6 months.
Setting: Musculoskeletal unit of an acute inpatient rehabilitation
hospital.
Participants: Patients in musculoskeletal rehabilitation and re-
ceiving opioids.
Interventions: None.
Main Outcome Measures: Total hours of therapy, total hours
of therapy missed, and hours of therapy missed due to illness.
Results: 8559 total hours of therapy; 179.25 hours of therapy
missed (2.09%); 69.75 hours of therapy missed due to illness
(0.81%).
Conclusions: Nearly 40% of missed therapy was due to adverse
effects in common with opioids. It is imperative that patients have
pain relief to perform therapy. However, it is not ideal to administer
medications with adverse effects that cause the patient to miss
therapy. Many patients were switched to medications such as tra-
madol, however, without the required pain relief to continue with
therapy. Another disadvantage of many opioids is the need for
multiple stool softeners, laxatives, and antiemetics. Tapentadol is a
new opioid pain reliever, which, in clinical trials, has shown fewer
gastrointestinal adverse effects. This study has prompted further
research to investigate the usefulness of a new opioid such as
tapentadol in the acute inpatient rehabilitation setting to limit the
amount of gastrointestinal adverse effects that led to half of all
missed therapy.
